<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Jabrid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1440 CE – 1524 CE (approx.) · capital al-Ahsa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bedouin dynasty that ruled eastern Arabia and the Bahrain islands in the 15th century until the Portuguese arrived.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Jabrids, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-amir">Banu Amir</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uqaylid-dynasty">Banu Uqayl</a>, were a nomadic clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>. Around 1440 they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Ahsa</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a> islands from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jarwanid-dynasty">Jarwanid dynasty</a>, establishing a Sunni dynasty based in al-Ahsa.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajwad-ibn-zamil">Ajwad ibn Zamil</a>, the greatest ruler, made his state the strongest in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, extended influence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and made the pilgrimage with a large retinue, receiving honours from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>. The Jabrids followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a>, sponsored scholars and controlled pearl fisheries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a>. Their relations with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Kingdom of Ormus</a> moved between alliance and rivalry over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1521 a Portuguese and Hormuzi force took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a>; the Jabrid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqrin-ibn-zamil">Muqrin ibn Zamil</a> was mortally wounded, beginning <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-bahrain">Portuguese Bahrain</a>. The dynasty kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Ahsa</a> a few more years before fading, after which the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> took the region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jabrid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Jabrid dynasty" width="320" height="264"><figcaption>Image: AbdurRahman AbdulMoneim · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jabrids_(Banu_Jabr).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1440 CE – 1524 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>al-Ahsa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ajwad ibn Zamil, Muqrin ibn Zamil</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>, <a href="/countries/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, <a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jabrids, Banu Jabr</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1482&amp;focus=jabrid-dynasty" aria-label="Jabrid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5871 1300 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jabrid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6318 1458h0M4773 1044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6191 1556h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6191 1556h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Saudi Arabia. <a href="/atlas?year=1482&amp;focus=jabrid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
